::MCR_U06_L05_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
What is the distance from (2, 5) to (9, 5)?
{
~A. 11 units#This adds the x-values. Horizontal distance is found by subtracting x-values.
=B. 7 units#Correct. The y-values match, so subtract x-values: 9 - 2 = 7.
~C. 4 units#This subtracts the y-values, but the y-values are the same.
~D. 9 units#This uses one coordinate as the distance instead of comparing both points.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
What is the distance from (4, 1) to (4, 8)?
{
~A. 4 units#This uses the shared x-value as the distance. Compare y-values for vertical distance.
~B. 9 units#This adds the y-values. Distance is the difference between positions.
~C. 3 units#This subtracts 4 and 1, but vertical distance uses the y-values 1 and 8.
=D. 7 units#Correct. The x-values match, so subtract y-values: 8 - 1 = 7.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
A rectangle has side lengths 6 units and 4 units. What is its perimeter?
{
=A. 20 units#Correct. Perimeter = 6 + 4 + 6 + 4 = 20 units.
~B. 10 units#This adds each different side only once. Perimeter includes all four sides.
~C. 24 square units#This multiplies side lengths and uses area units.
~D. 20 square units#The number is correct, but perimeter uses linear units, not square units.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
A rectangle has vertices (1, 2), (6, 2), (6, 5), and (1, 5). What is its area?
{
~A. 8 square units#This adds width and height. Area requires multiplication.
~B. 16 square units#This is the perimeter, not the area.
=C. 15 square units#Correct. Width = 6 - 1 = 5 and height = 5 - 2 = 3, so area = 15.
~D. 15 units#The number is correct, but area needs square units.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
Which coordinate changes when moving vertically on the coordinate plane?
{
~A. x-coordinate#The x-coordinate changes when moving horizontally.
=B. y-coordinate#Correct. Vertical movement changes the y-coordinate.
~C. both coordinates must change#Vertical movement keeps the x-coordinate the same.
~D. neither coordinate changes#If neither coordinate changes, the point does not move.
}
